<p>An Israeli court in Ashkelon extended the detention of Spanish national Saif Abu Keshek and Brazilian activist Thiago Avila by two days after Israeli forces intercepted a Gaza-bound flotilla in international waters off Greece early Thursday and took them to Israel for questioning, the rights group Adalah said. The flotilla of more than 50 vessels set sail from France, Spain and Italy to challenge Israel’s blockade of Gaza and bring supplies to the Palestinian territory; Israel said about 175 activists were removed, with two transferred to Israel for questioning. Adalah said Israeli authorities presented suspected offenses including “assisting the enemy during wartime” and “membership in and providing services to a terrorist organisation.” Lawyers for the two men challenged Israeli jurisdiction and called the seizure an unlawful abduction in international waters. Israel’s Foreign Ministry said the two activists were linked to the Popular Conference for Palestinians Abroad, which it described as a Hamas-linked group sanctioned by the United States, and said Abu Keshek and Avila would receive consular visits. Lawyers for the two men told the court the activists reported severe physical abuse, including beatings, isolation and being blindfolded for days at sea. Israeli Foreign Ministry spokesman Oren Marmorstein rejected the torture allegations as “false and baseless,” saying Israeli personnel used force after Abu Keshek violently obstructed authorities.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/qzc7NOnkpQqCf6zNZUViPC1s8C_F95IKNWdKVG1LSwZHWwAaMcy8winNXPruVJzh4sO2Mx2mvCKzLnVqE2gxhhf4H8fkMwsufGk_tDLTD0EirlVTwzhOe5ZYLpuW5gjhN7mcTB6vyQ4vF_pHkYPi0SN4vwCb_3DyeVIHvKSuljBFIJDa-9LMbPD2oYYzLXGsHA3FG5bz-u-TB901h1yjRGxmoPcCplcdlXnUJB-NtTUdOD_eU0Ox2bj7ErMtqNjXRwYQ' alt='Israel prolonged the detention of a Spanish-Palestinian activist and a Brazilian activist after intercepting a humanitarian flotilla.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Court request: Israeli prosecutors requested a four-day extension for further questioning, a longer period than the court allowed.</li><li>No charges: Adalah said no formal charges had been filed against either activist and that they remained at the pre-indictment interrogation stage.</li><li>Custody location: Adalah said Avila and Abu Keshek would be transferred back to the Shikma detention facility in Ashkelon after the hearing.</li><li>Other activists: Dutch public broadcaster NOS said two Dutch flotilla participants arrived at Schiphol after the interception, while a third Dutch participant remained on Crete, according to the Global Sumud Flotilla.</li></ul><blockquote>I had not expected such a warm welcome - Osman Tastan</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Adalah: The legal center said its lawyers visited the two detainees in prison before the hearing and used the court session to oppose any further interrogation period. (<a href='https://www.iltempo.it/esteri/2026/05/03/news/flotilla-i-due-attivisti-arrestati-restano-in-carcere-israele-hanno-collegamenti-con-hamas--47552088/'>Il Tempo</a>)</li><li>Israeli Foreign Ministry: The ministry described the flotilla as a provocation and said Jerusalem would continue enforcing what it called its lawful naval blockade. (<a href='https://www.jns.org/news/israel-news/leaders-of-gaza-bound-flotilla-arrive-in-israel-after-naval-interception-near-greece'>JNS</a>)</li><li>Spain: El País reported that Spain’s Congress would hold an extraordinary plenary session on the case after Foreign Minister José Manuel Albares agreed to appear. (<a href='https://elpais.com/internacional/2026-05-03/israel-prolonga-la-detencion-de-los-activistas-espanol-y-brasileno-de-la-flotilla-de-ayuda-a-gaza.html'>El País</a>)</li><li>Global Sumud Flotilla: The activist group urged Spain and Brazil to act immediately, called for independent public investigations into the arrests and demanded sanctions against Israel over what it described as violations of international law. (<a href='https:/